.TH "vsearch-history" "7" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
history \[em] release history of vsearch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
This page is the release history of \f[B]vsearch\f[R].
It records the new features and the important modifications of each
release, and nothing else; see \f[V]vsearch(1)\f[R] for the list of
commands.
.SH VERSION HISTORY
.PP
New features and important modifications of \f[B]vsearch\f[R], most
recent first (short-lived or minor bug releases may not be mentioned):
.TP
\f[B]v2.32.0\f[R] released September 18th, 2026
Includes the following changes:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--gapopen\f[R]/\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[V]*\f[R] now forbid gaps
at the match/merge decision, not only in the alignment score: a
\f[V]*\f[R] opening penalty forbids that gap class outright, a
\f[V]*\f[R] extension penalty forbids gaps longer than one, and both
apply per sequence (Q/T) and location (L/I/R/E).
For instance \f[V]--gapopen \[aq]*LQ\[aq]\f[R] forbids opening left
end-gaps in the query, and a bare \f[V]--gapopen \[aq]*\[aq]\f[R]
forbids every gap opening, including the terminal gaps used for
substring matching.
Previously \f[V]*\f[R] only lowered the score, so a pair whose best
alignment still needed a forbidden gap was reported as a match (issue
#602, and a 2016 forum report of internal gaps appearing despite
\f[V]--gapopen \[aq]*I\[aq]\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: stop truncating the \f[V]*\f[R] infinite gap penalty on the SIMD
path, so \f[V]--gapopen\f[R]/\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[V]*\f[R] forbid gaps
on short sequences too, not just on long ones.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: stop truncating abundance to 32 bits when writing size annotations.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: stop truncating abundances above 2\[ha]32 in the
\f[V]--sortbysize\f[R] and \f[V]--sortbylength\f[R] decks, where the
most abundant sequences sorted to the bottom (\f[V];size=4294967297\f[R]
truncated to 1, ranking below \f[V];size=100\f[R]) and \[lq]Median
abundance:\[rq] reported a median of the truncated values.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] with \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 64 and
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] between 63 and 93 wrote quality symbols
outside printable ASCII (byte 127 at qmaxout 63, up to 149 at 93),
producing an invalid FASTQ file, and a SIGSEGV in v2.31.0, which indexes
a table with the resulting negative char.
The sum rule was stated against \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R], which is the
one fastq-writing command that does not accept it.
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] was rejected for the mirror-image reason and
is now accepted where the real offset represents it.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: the k-mer pre-filter required a target to share more k-mers than it
can hold, so a database sequence with less than
\f[V]wordlength + minwordmatches - 1\f[R] = 19 nt of distinct k-mer
content could not be selected by any query, an exact match included, and
the same pair gave opposite answers depending on direction (issue #328).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastx_uniques --strand both --fastqout\f[R] merged a
minus-strand member\[cq]s quality string positionally against the
plus-strand seed, averaging seed position \f[I]i\f[R] with member
position \f[I]i\f[R] instead of \f[I]seqlen - 1 - i\f[R].
Output changes versus released binaries for strand-both fastq
dereplication.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastx_uniques --fastqout\f[R] truncated merged qualities
through an irreversible probability round trip: quality 2 came back as
1, and qualities 3, 5, 6, 8, 10 and 17 moved with the abundances (two
identical Q10 reads merged to Q10 at abundances 1+1, but to Q9 at 1+2).
Present since v2.20.0.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--selfid\f[R] never excluded anything on any chimera path.
The gate compared a query \f[I]part\f[R], always shorter than a
full-length identical reference, so the length pre-check
short-circuited, and an identical reference in the database won every
smoothing window and suppressed detection entirely.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--cluster_unoise\f[R] silently ignored a user-supplied
\f[V]--weak_id\f[R], overwriting it with 0.90 unconditionally; 0.90 is
now a true default.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: count \f[V]--qsegout\f[R], \f[V]--tsegout\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastapairs\f[R] as output files in the \[lq]No output files
specified\[rq] gates of \f[V]--allpairs_global\f[R],
\f[V]--usearch_global\f[R], \f[V]--search_exact\f[R] and the
\f[V]--cluster_*\f[R] commands, so each can be used as the sole output.
Same defect and same fix as issue #82.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: enforce the documented ranges of option arguments.
A negative \f[V]--idprefix\f[R]/\f[V]--idsuffix\f[R] crashed
(out-of-bounds read under NDEBUG);
\f[V]--query_cov\f[R]/\f[V]--target_cov\f[R] outside 0.0-1.0 silently
made the filter unsatisfiable or inert; negative \f[V]--maxdiffs\f[R],
\f[V]--maxsubs\f[R], \f[V]--maxgaps\f[R], \f[V]--mincols\f[R],
\f[V]--maxqsize\f[R], \f[V]--mintsize\f[R] and \f[V]--topn\f[R] silently
rejected every hit or ignored the option entirely;
\f[V]--maxrejects -1\f[R] was indistinguishable from not passing the
option; and an empty argument such as \f[V]--id \[dq]\[dq]\f[R] was read
as zero, accepting every hit.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: accept a correctly-rounded underflowing double such as
\f[V]--fastq_maxee 1e-320\f[R], which died with \[lq]Illegal option
argument\[rq] although the manual admits any positive real.
Overflow stays rejected.
Whether \f[V]strtod\f[R] even sets ERANGE on underflow is
implementation-defined, so the old rejection also varied with the
platform\[cq]s libc.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastx_getseqs --label_word \[dq]\[dq]\f[R] matched every
record.
An empty needle gave \f[V]std::strstr\f[R] no exit condition, so the
search walked past the end of the header into the zero-filled buffer
tail.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R]/\f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] silently ignored
\f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R] on fasta input instead of rejecting it; the
comparison tested \f[V]< LONG_MIN\f[R], which no value satisfies, where
\f[V]!= LONG_MIN\f[R] was meant.
The fatal message has always listed it among the not-accepted options.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: a second one-past-the-end abundance read in
\f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R] (\f[V]random_subsampling\f[R]), in the
priming read before the loop, reached on an empty database.
The read inside the loop was guarded by PR #650; this is the one four
lines above it.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] wrote a UDB declaring zero sequences
and exited 0, then rejected that file itself on every subsequent read
with \[lq]Invalid UDB file\[rq].
Reachable through \f[V]--minseqlength\f[R], not only through an empty
input file.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: extend the UDB load validation of PR #647 to \f[V]--udbinfo\f[R],
which reads the header through its own path and so accepted both a UDB
served from a pipe and an inflated stored sequence count, printing a
garbage count and exiting 0.
A stored word count larger than the database is now rejected by every
reader too: it cannot describe a valid word list, and such slots were
never visited while still being reported.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--udb2fasta\f[R], \f[V]--udbstats\f[R] and \f[V]--udbinfo\f[R]
no longer print \[lq]WARNING: Wordlength adjusted to N as indicated in
UDB file\[rq].
None of the three accepts \f[V]--wordlength\f[R], so the warning
announced a change to a setting the user had no way of making; for
\f[V]--udbstats\f[R] the report prints the word length two lines later
anyway.
Search commands, which do have a configured value for the file to
override, keep it.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: uppercase the reference bases in SAM \f[V]MD\f[R] fields, as the
SAMtags grammar requires.
A target lowercased by DUST masking produced \f[V]MD:Z:20a19\f[R] and
\f[V]MD:Z:20\[ha]a19\f[R], which disagree with what samtools calmd
recomputes for the same alignment, and with the file itself after a BAM
round trip.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--sff_convert\f[R] read 32 header bytes where the format
specifies 31 (the struct carries a trailing padding byte), shifting
every later section by one: the reported key sequence lost its first
base, and spec-valid files were wrongly rejected whenever
\f[V]31 + flows + key\f[R] is a multiple of 8.
The per-record offsets re-synchronized through the padding arithmetic,
so the fastq output itself was always correct.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R] no longer disables the transparent read
that lets uncompressed standard input through.
Feeding plain input to the option died with \[lq]incorrect header
check\[rq] instead of reading it as-is, against zlib 1.3.2 as shipped by
FreeBSD.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastq_stats\f[R] searched past the end of its per-position
tables for the first complete EE filtering row.
The bound is \f[V]len_max\f[R], as in the pre-refactoring report loop.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastq_eestats2\f[R] printed \[lq]0 reads, max len 0, avg
-nan\[rq] to \f[V]--log\f[R] on empty input, where \f[V]--output\f[R]
printed \[lq]0 reads\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: restore one progress line per percent step when standard error is
not a terminal.
The behaviour added in v2.31.0 was silently reverted when the callers
migrated to the progress class, which had been written from the older
code.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: warnings now reach the \f[V]--log\f[R] file.
Two of the fifteen warning sites (\f[V]--sff_convert\f[R]\[cq]s missing
index padding, the UDB adjusted-wordlength notice) wrote to standard
error and stopped there, and the \f[V]--rereplicate\f[R] and
sequence-retrieval warnings obeyed \f[V]--quiet\f[R] on standard error
while logging unconditionally.
The manual says \f[V]--quiet\f[R] suppresses everything \[lq]except for
warnings and error messages\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--chimeras_parents_max\f[R] above 20 was an out-of-bounds heap
write, and \f[V]--iddef\f[R] outside 0-4 selected an undefined identity
definition, for a caller reaching the engines through the library: both
were range-checked in the command-line parser only.
Both moved to the shared validator, and the
\f[V]parents_found <= 20\f[R] assert became a runtime check so it holds
under NDEBUG.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: reject \f[V]-\f[R] given as both the query and \f[V]--db\f[R].
Since \f[V]--db\f[R] gained standard-input support the two readers raced
over the same stream and the command silently returned no hits with exit
0 (issue #506).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--orient\f[R] against a database of fewer than eight sequences
gave a bitmap to every one of the 4\[ha]wordlength index slots, absent
k-mers included, because integer division makes the threshold zero: +512
MB of peak memory and a full SIMD bitmap scan per absent k-mer (0.85 s
to 0.25 s on a 4-sequence database).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: undefined behaviour at twelve \f[V]<cctype>\f[R] call sites passing
a plain \f[V]char\f[R], reachable wherever \f[V]char\f[R] is signed
(x86-64, Windows) through sequence headers carrying UTF-8 or Latin-1.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] no longer reports two discard reasons
that could never occur: \[lq]indel errors\[rq], unassigned since it was
added in 2017 because an ungapped aligner cannot detect an indel, and
\[lq]undefined reason\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: the FASTQ offset-warning threshold counted 101 records where the
constant and its comment both say 100.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--help\f[R] now lists the commands \f[V]--fastx_getseq\f[R],
\f[V]--fastx_getseqs\f[R] and \f[V]--fastx_getsubseq\f[R], under a new
\[lq]Sequence retrieval\[rq] section, along with the options specific to
them (\f[V]--label\f[R], \f[V]--labels\f[R], \f[V]--label_word\f[R],
\f[V]--label_words\f[R], \f[V]--label_field\f[R],
\f[V]--label_substr_match\f[R], \f[V]--subseq_start\f[R],
\f[V]--subseq_end\f[R] and \f[V]--notmatchedfq\f[R]).
The three commands have manual pages but could not be discovered from
the help text.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: \f[V]--help\f[R] now lists the output annotation options
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--xlength\f[R] and \f[V]--sample\f[R]
wherever it already lists their siblings \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] and
\f[V]--xsize\f[R], lists \f[V]--allow_fewer\f[R] under
\f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R], and lists \f[V]--fastq_qout_max\f[R], the
last of the twenty-nine options \f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R] accepts that
appeared nowhere in the help text.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: out-of-bounds read in \f[V]--fastx_getsubseq\f[R] when
\f[V]--subseq_start\f[R] exceeds a sequence\[cq]s length, in
mixed-length files (also affects the \f[V]--fastqout\f[R] path) (PR
#650).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: one-past-the-end abundance read in \f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R]
(\f[V]random_subsampling\f[R]), reached with \f[V]--sizein\f[R] when the
last selected read is the last read of the last amplicon (PR #650).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: hit-list buffer overflow in clustering with large
\f[V]--maxaccepts\f[R]/\f[V]--maxrejects\f[R] on small datasets (PR
#650).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: harden the SFF reader (\f[V]--sff_convert\f[R]) against truncated
or corrupt files: inverted clip regions under \f[V]--sff_clip\f[R],
flowgram truncation, and overflow guards that were previously compiled
out of release builds (PR #650).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: bound-check the CIGAR walk in SAM output (\f[V]--samout\f[R]) to
prevent out-of-bounds reads on malformed or mismatched alignments (PR
#649).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: reject non-finite floating-point command-line arguments such as
\f[V]--sample_pct\f[R] nan or inf, which previously slipped past range
checks (PR #649).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: on Windows, load the zlib and bzip2 DLLs with
\f[V]LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DEFAULT_DIRS\f[R] so a planted
\f[V]zlib1.dll\f[R]/\f[V]libbz2.dll\f[R] in the working directory can no
longer be loaded on the first \f[V].gz\f[R]/\f[V].bz2\f[R] input (PR
#649).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: validate UDB header fields on load (kmerindex entries,
kmerindexsize, seqcount, header offsets); corrupt or crafted
\f[V].udb\f[R] files are now rejected with \[lq]Invalid UDB file\[rq]
instead of causing out-of-bounds memory access; valid files are
unaffected (PR #647).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: avoid int overflow when sizing and indexing the
\f[V]--fastq_eestats2\f[R] count table with a large
\f[V]--length_cutoffs\f[R] range and many \f[V]--ee_cutoffs\f[R] values
(PR #644).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: avoid intermittent crashes from terminating in a worker thread on
malformed or mismatched input (PR #635, #636).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: data race on the query file position in \f[V]--uchime_ref\f[R] with
multiple threads (PR #637).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: print FASTA sequences longer than \f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] (about 2 GB,
reachable with a raised \f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R]) correctly instead of
silently truncating them, and reject FASTA/FASTQ headers longer than the
int length limit instead of overflowing the query buffer (PR #643).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: bound sequence length at the central FASTA/FASTQ reader and lower
the \f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] hard cap to \f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] - 2001
.RS 2
.IP "(2147481646)" 13
to prevent int overflow in the search, cluster and chimera engines (PR
#646; refines the 2\[ha]32-1 cap from PR #632).
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: detect write, flush and close errors on output streams; a deferred
write failure (full disk, exceeded quota, read-only remount) now aborts
with a fatal error instead of silently producing a truncated output file
that exits 0 (PR #651).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: duplicated annotation separators (\f[V];\f[R]) in fastq and fasta
headers (issue #271).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: shared-kmer counter overflow when aligning very long sequences
(about 75 kbp) (PR #630).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: SIMD alignment counters (aligned/matches/mismatches/gaps) could
overflow when the alignment path exceeds 65535 columns; over-long
sequence pairs are now diverted to the linear-memory aligner, with
64-bit length arithmetic (PR #631).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: handle sequence abundances above 2\[ha]31 (\f[V];size=\f[R]) in
storage, filtering and output (including \f[V];seqs=\f[R] and centroid
size) and in the library API; the abskew/size-ratio comparison is now
exact above 2\[ha]53, and \f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] rejects values above
2\[ha]32-1 (PR #632).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: commands \f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R], \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_eestats\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] write
\f[V]qmin\f[R] to the log file instead of standard error.
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: off-by-one in MSA consensus \f[V];length=\f[R] output (PR #629).
.IP \[bu] 2
fix: command \f[V]--rereplicate\f[R] writes its warning to the log file
instead of standard error (PR #628).
.IP \[bu] 2
add: new command \f[V]--scramble\f[R]: randomize the nucleotide order
within each fasta or fastq entry, the within-entry counterpart of
\f[V]--shuffle\f[R].
Useful for building null-model datasets: entry order, headers, lengths,
and per-entry nucleotide composition are preserved; fastq quality
strings are copied through unchanged.
Reproducible with \f[V]--randseed\f[R].
The option \f[V]--scramble_kmer\f[R] \f[I]k\f[R] (1 to 9, default 1)
additionally preserves the counts of all words of length \f[I]k\f[R] or
less, by sampling a uniformly random Eulerian path of each entry\[cq]s
de Bruijn graph (Altschul-Erickson/uShuffle-style).
.IP \[bu] 2
add: ten new \f[V]--userfields\f[R] output fields (issue #548):
\f[V]diffs\f[R] and \f[V]mid\f[R], which report the quantities
\f[V]--maxdiffs\f[R] and \f[V]--mid\f[R] are compared against;
\f[V]qseq\f[R] and \f[V]tseq\f[R], the full-length query and target
sequences; \f[V]qrowdots\f[R] and \f[V]trowdots\f[R], the aligned
segments with a dot at every position identical to the other sequence;
and \f[V]qlor\f[R], \f[V]qhir\f[R], \f[V]tlor\f[R] and \f[V]thir\f[R],
the alignment span in 0-based coordinates.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: new option \f[V]--allow_fewer\f[R] for \f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R],
which reads \f[V]--sample_size\f[R] as an upper bound: take at most N
reads, and keep them all when fewer are available.
Without it, a subset size larger than the input remains an error, as in
usearch.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: new option \f[V]--fastq_solexa\f[R] for \f[V]--fastq_convert\f[R],
which converts Solexa scores (-10 log10(p / (1 - p))) to Phred scores.
The two scales share the ASCII offset 64 and nothing else, so a Solexa
file was previously rebased and left otherwise untouched: fifteen of the
68 representable symbols came out wrong, and they were exactly the low
ones a quality filter acts on.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a warning when the quality symbols read contradict
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R].
Raising \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] means a phred+64 file read at the default
offset 33 is no longer stopped by the bound, so vsearch now says so
instead.
Run \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] to confirm.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a warning when database sequences yield no k-mer for the index
(issue #570).
Such sequences are absent from the index and are never selected as
candidates, so an all-lowercase reference database silently produced
empty classifications.
Emitted once per run as a summary line, not once per sequence.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a warning when a minimum threshold exceeds its matching maximum
(\f[V]--minuniquesize\f[R]/\f[V]--maxuniquesize\f[R],
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R]/\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R]).
Such a pair selects nothing, and the command used to write an empty
output file and exit 0; the empty output stays legal.
Also catches the implicit inversion, for instance
\f[V]--maxseqlength 10\f[R] alone against the default minimum of
.RS 2
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a warning when \f[V]--sample\f[R] resolves to an empty sample name,
which writes a bare \f[V];sample=\f[R] annotation and yields an OTU
table whose first column has no name.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a warning when \f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R] or
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R] contradicts a seekable standard input,
where autodetection runs and wins and the asserted format is ignored.
On piped standard input the same mistake is already fatal.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: shell auto-completion scripts for bash, zsh and fish (issue #417),
installed by \f[V]make install\f[R] in the standard location for each
shell.
Completion offers the commands, then only the options the chosen command
accepts, values for options taking a fixed set, and filenames filtered
by extension.
Configure with \f[V]--disable-completion\f[R] or
\f[V]--with-bashcompdir=DIR\f[R] and friends.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: an online manual, published per command, with the released pages at
\f[V]/vsearch/\f[R] and the development pages under
\f[V]/vsearch/dev/\f[R].
\f[V]--help\f[R] now points at both.
.IP \[bu] 2
add: a \f[V]--enable-sanitize\f[R] configure switch that builds the
vsearch executable under the address and undefined-behaviour sanitizers,
independently of \f[V]--enable-debug\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
add: new command \f[V]--fastx_syncpairs\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
add: preliminary support for FreeBSD/x86-64 (PR #633, #634).
.IP \[bu] 2
add: new option \f[V]--centroid_sizeout\f[R], which reports the centroid
size in the FASTA header after clustering (issue #623).
.IP \[bu] 2
remove: \f[V]--search_exact\f[R] no longer accepts \f[V]--lcaout\f[R]
and \f[V]--lca_cutoff\f[R].
It never implemented them, in this tree or upstream: no call to the LCA
writer exists on that code path, so the run completed, exited 0, and
created no file.
\f[V]--usearch_global\f[R], the only command that implements LCA output,
is unaffected.
.IP \[bu] 2
remove: the monolithic \f[V]vsearch.1\f[R] manual page, deprecated since
the per-command pages and the online manual took over.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] now default
to the highest quality score the offset can represent (93 with
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33, 62 with offset 64) instead of 41.
PacBio HiFi reaches Q93 and nanopore basecallers pass Q50, and the old
bound rejected those files outright (issues #522, #194 and #609).
Pass \f[V]--fastq_qmax 41\f[R] for the previous behaviour.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fasta2fastq\f[R] and \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] keep
the previous \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] default of 41, because they
generate the score they clamp rather than passing one through:
\f[V]--fasta2fastq\f[R] has no input quality, and
\f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] caps a computed posterior (two agreeing Q40
bases would otherwise be reported as Q85 instead of Q41).
Pass \f[V]--fastq_qmaxout 93\f[R] for the unclamped values.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: the default thread count, and an explicit \f[V]--threads 0\f[R],
now follow the cores this run may actually use, namely the CPU affinity
mask and the cgroup CPU quota, instead of the machine\[cq]s core count;
memory detection is cgroup-aware for the same reason.
Inside a Slurm, Docker, podman, Kubernetes or Apptainer confinement
vsearch read the host\[cq]s figures: a session limited to 50 MB and one
core was told it had 500+ GB and 128 cores, and then really did start
128 workers on that one core (issue #584).
A virtual machine was already correct and is unaffected.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastq_stats\f[R] now requires \f[V]--log\f[R], as the
manual documents; the report has no other destination.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] now honours \f[V]--quiet\f[R] for
its final report on standard error, diverging from the issue #527
resolution, and \f[V]--log\f[R] no longer takes that report away from
standard error: every other command writes both copies.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] applies \f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R]
after quality truncation, as the shared option fragment (\[lq]Applied
after trimming\[rq]) and the discard-reason string (\[lq]reads too long
(after truncation)\[rq]) both already stated.
A pair truncated to within \f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R] is no longer
discarded as too long.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R] now applies \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] to its input.
Both were listed among its valid options and neither was enforced,
although the command decodes every quality symbol, converts it to an
error probability, takes an abundance-weighted mean across the cluster
and re-encodes the result.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--derep_prefix\f[R] now requires an output option, like
\f[V]--derep_fulllength\f[R] and \f[V]--derep_id\f[R].
A run with neither \f[V]--output\f[R] nor \f[V]--uc\f[R] used to read
the input, write nothing, and exit 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R] and \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] now reject
a \f[V]*_rev\f[R] output given without \f[V]--reverse\f[R], for which
the file was never opened: such a run created no file, wrote nothing,
and exited 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--udb2fasta --sizeout\f[R] now needs \f[V]--sizein\f[R] to
reproduce the abundances stored in the UDB, following the convention of
every other command.
Previously \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] alone overwrote every abundance with
\f[V]size=1\f[R], so the option that asks for the annotation was the one
destroying it, and \f[V]--sizein\f[R] had no effect on this path.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: \f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] masks on several threads instead of
being forced to one and warning.
Only the masking phase is parallel; reading, indexing and writing the
UDB remain serial.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: four commands accept options they already needed.
\f[V]--cut\f[R] accepts \f[V]--threads\f[R] (it warns and forces one
thread, like every other single-threaded command);
\f[V]--fastx_mask\f[R], \f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R] and
\f[V]--orient\f[R] accept
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R]/\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R], whose input was
silently capped at the 50000-nt default with no way to raise it;
\f[V]--derep_fulllength\f[R] accepts \f[V]--label_suffix\f[R], like its
four sibling dereplication commands; and \f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R]
accepts \f[V]--tabbedout\f[R] with fasta input, since none of the six
tabbedout columns depends on quality data.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: the \f[V]--sintax --randseed\f[R] multithreading warning is
removed.
The per-query RNG is seeded from a per-query substream, so the
classification is reproducible whatever the thread count, exactly as the
manual page states.
.IP \[bu] 2
change: reject \f[V]--match\f[R]/\f[V]--mismatch\f[R] outside
-32767..32767 and finite \f[V]--gapopen\f[R]/\f[V]--gapext\f[R] outside
0..6553, instead of silently wrapping them to the 16-bit aligner.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R] and \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] are about
2x faster (552 ms to 282 ms on 300000 records, byte-identical).
Most of the gain is in the shared FASTQ reader, so every FASTQ-reading
command benefits: \f[V]--fastq_stats\f[R] -27%,
\f[V]--fastq_eestats2\f[R] -15%, \f[V]--fastq_join\f[R] -13%,
\f[V]--fastq_convert\f[R] -13%, \f[V]--fastx_revcomp\f[R] -12%,
\f[V]--fastx_subsample\f[R] -11%, \f[V]--fastq_chars\f[R] -11%,
\f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R] -8%.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] is 3.0x faster on one thread and
1.9x on eight, and 3.5x on a non-overlapping pair set, chiefly by
indexing its 5-mers directly (1024 possible values) instead of hashing
them.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--search_exact\f[R] is 1.74x faster at
\f[V]--threads 24\f[R] and uses 61 MB instead of 3752 MB, by growing the
per-thread hit buffer on demand rather than sizing it to the whole
database, per strand, per thread.
That allocation, not the search, was what made the command slower with
more threads.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--sintax\f[R] is 15% to 38% faster depending on how
query-heavy the run is: byte-wide hit counters (a sintax count cannot
exceed 32, so a 16-bit counter is twice the width it needs) and a block
skip over the counters that cannot beat the running best, which is
99.94% of them.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--udb2fasta\f[R] is 7% to 82% faster and uses 21 MB
instead of 1.0 GB on a word length 13 database, by not loading a k-mer
index it never reads.
\f[V]--udbstats\f[R] no longer sorts the whole k-mer table to print
eleven rows and two order statistics (up to -91%), and no longer builds
that report when \f[V]--log\f[R] is absent and it cannot be printed.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] is 35% faster (4.93 s to 3.18 s on
the PR2 reference): parallel masking, and a word-at-a-time scan that
expands a bitmapped k-mer 8.6x faster than bit by bit.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--uchime_denovo\f[R] and the \f[V]--uchime*_ref\f[R]
commands are 13% faster: clearing only the live prefix of the match and
insert arrays (about 9 rows of the 400 allocated) and wiping each match
column once instead of once per overlapping window.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--fastx_syncpairs\f[R] executes 16% fewer instructions and
allocates far less, storing the reverse reads in a chunked arena instead
of three separate allocations per record.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--fastx_getseqs --labels\f[R] hashes the labels for exact
whole-header matching instead of comparing every record with every
label: 1.95 billion string comparisons for 10000 labels against 200000
records, 90% of the command\[cq]s runtime, and quadratic growth on both
axes.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: DUST masking skips the start positions that provably cannot
score, which is 99.4% of windows on real data.
.IP \[bu] 2
improve: \f[V]--fastq_eestats\f[R] can now process long reads in a
memory-efficient way.
